-- #1094: WHY INVARIANCE AND NOT COVARIANCE.  This is the direction a `≤`-covariance
-- rule would license (`<> ≤ <Stdout>`, widening a PURE index) — and it is unsound,
-- because a CONTRAVARIANT `Effect`-indexed type is expressible today once its kind is
-- declared: `Sink`'s `e` occurs only in the DOMAIN of the stored function.
--
-- Widening `Sink <>` to `Sink <Stdout>` hands `runSink` a licence to pass an effectful
-- callback into a sink built for pure ones, so
--     runSink widened shout : Unit
-- is a binding with no effect row that prints.  Since a parameter's polarity is not
-- knowable without a variance analysis (which this compiler does not have), the safe
-- direction for a covariant occurrence is exactly the unsafe one here: only EQUALITY
-- is sound at an index.  Sibling of effect_index_written_row_invariant.mdk, which
-- pins the opposite direction on a covariant index.
data Sink (e : Effect) = MkSink ((Unit -> <e> Unit) -> Unit)

pureSink : Sink <>
pureSink = MkSink (g => g ())

widened : Sink <Stdout>
widened = pureSink
